00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:11.400
The richest country in the world is Qatar
which looks like this.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.590
The second richest country is Luxembourg which
looks like this.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:18.580
The third richest is Singapore which looks
like this.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:24.270
The commonality between these three is that
each of them has more than 90% of their population

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:25.830
living in cities.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:29.640
The three poorest countries—the Central
African Republic, the Democratic Republic

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.950
of the Congo, and Burundi—each have urbanization
levels below 50%.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:39.399
Comparing the wealth of African countries
and the urbanization levels of African countries,

00:00:39.399 --> 00:00:41.469
the maps are nearly identical.

00:00:41.469 --> 00:00:46.959
This is not to say that cities automatically
create wealth or wealth automatically creates

00:00:46.959 --> 00:00:50.579
cities, but there is a direct correlation
between the two.

00:00:50.579 --> 00:00:55.679
Although, it seems strange that humans would
choose to live in cities—living costs are

00:00:55.679 --> 00:00:59.850
higher, air quality is worse, surroundings
are more crowded, crime is more prevalent,

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.199
and disease is more easily spread.

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:06.729
If the population of the world spread itself
out evenly, every single person would have

00:01:06.729 --> 00:01:09.340
5 acres to live on, but we don’t.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:15.010
We live in a world where half the population
lives on just 1% of the land.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:19.990
54% of the world population now lives in cities,
and that’s significant.

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:23.590
55 years ago, only half that lived a city
life.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:29.869
The urbanization of the world is changing
the world, but why do cities even exist?

00:01:29.869 --> 00:01:31.990
Humans were originally hunter-gatherers.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:35.781
They scavenged for food and often didn’t
live in a set place—similar to how many

00:01:35.781 --> 00:01:40.469
animals live—but then in about 10,000 BC
we learned to farm.

00:01:40.469 --> 00:01:45.450
The domestication of plants allowed humans
to finally settle down in one singular place

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:50.270
and establish a long-term home, but farming
was nothing like it is today.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:53.760
The humans of the era lacked technology and
expertise.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.270
The first archeological evidence of plowed
fields—a process that greatly improves crop

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:01.359
yields—doesn’t appear until about 3000
BC.

00:02:01.359 --> 00:02:06.289
It took thousands more years for farmers to
successfully domesticate animals like the

00:02:06.289 --> 00:02:07.729
ox to pull a plow.

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:12.580
When humans first began farming, nearly every
individual had to farm in order to make enough

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:13.799
food to live.

00:02:13.799 --> 00:02:18.920
One person’s work essentially produced one
person’s food, but through time as technological

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.580
advancements occurred, progressively each
individual was able to make more and more

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:24.580
food.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:30.019
Thanks to that, farmers today each feed 155
people.

00:02:30.019 --> 00:02:35.269
The process has also become so efficient that
only one acre of land is now needed to feed

00:02:35.269 --> 00:02:39.760
one person, but back in the early days of
farming, once technology had advanced to the

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.260
point where one person made more than one
person’s food, for the first time ever,

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:49.120
there were some people who didn’t need to
farm, and that fact changed humanity.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.700
If that had never happened, we would all still
be living in ancient history.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.540
The surplus of food and the development of
cities really went hand-in-hand.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:02.799
It had never before been possible for humans
to live in dense urban conglomerations because

00:03:02.799 --> 00:03:07.430
they needed land to farm, but these extra
people freed from farming were able to work

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:13.220
jobs other than farming which led to languages,
literature, science, and really all the technological

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:16.000
advancements that made our modern world.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.610
The spatial patterns of humans really happen
quite naturally.

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:24.521
Enough people are each making their decision
on where to live that, in aggregate, humans

00:03:24.521 --> 00:03:27.110
are going to live in the best places to live.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:32.329
There’s a sort of process of natural selection
that happens as billions of humans each pick

00:03:32.329 --> 00:03:34.579
their optimal place to live.

00:03:34.579 --> 00:03:39.140
Nothing is manufactured about where people
live and numbers back this up.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:43.629
You see, cities tend to stay at the same rank
throughout history.

00:03:43.629 --> 00:03:46.659
In 1800 the largest city in Europe was London.

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:49.769
Today, the largest city in Europe is still
London.

00:03:49.769 --> 00:03:55.569
In 1800 the second largest city in Europe
was Paris and it too remains second today.

00:03:55.569 --> 00:04:00.500
Cities grow, but those that were large in
the past tend to stay large, at least without

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:02.330
external influence.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:07.030
Cities don’t just change ranking on their
own, they change rank if something else changes.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:11.829
Hong Kong, for example, changed rank when
a change in sovereignty to Britain drew people

00:04:11.829 --> 00:04:17.130
in while with San Francisco the discovery
of natural resources, particularly gold, increased

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:22.320
growth rates while with Washington DC, the
coming of a new industry, the US Government,

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.320
changed the rank.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:28.050
If you were to just put a pause on all activity
but humans moving, rankings would hypothetically

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:33.420
stay exactly the same, but that’s not even
the best evidence for how natural the existence

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:34.750
of cities is.

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:39.700
According to US Census Bureau, the largest
city in the US is New York with 8.5 million

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:44.250
urban residents then the second is Los Angeles
with 4 million—roughly half as many as New

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:50.870
York—then third is Chicago with 2.7 million—roughly
a third—then Houston at 2.3—roughly a

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:54.580
fourth—then Phoenix at 1.6—roughly a fifth.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:05:00.190
Each city’s size is almost exactly determined
by the largest city’s size divided by it’s

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:01.190
rank.

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:05.330
It is uncanny how closely city sizes in countries
follow this distribution.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:07.620
It’s not just with the US.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:13.380
Germany’s largest city, Berlin, has 3.5
million residents, then Hamburg has 1.8 million,

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:18.570
almost exactly half, then Munich has slightly
more than a third at 1.4 million, then Cologne

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:23.750
has just a bit more than a forth at 1 million,
then Frankfurt has almost exactly a fifth

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:25.000
at 700,000.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:30.090
Now, there are anomalies—mostly countries
with recent rapid population growth—but

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:34.990
in a large number of countries, the ranking
of cities can be determined by this law, but

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:38.330
what’s significant is what else can be determined
with this law.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:42.800
If you take a book and rank the frequency
of words it follows this distribution.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:47.840
If you take a stadium of people and rank them
by income, it follows this distribution.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:53.280
If you take the sounds a dolphin makes and
rank them by frequency, it too follows this

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:54.280
distribution.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:59.030
This link between a distribution found in
nature and the size of cities helps prove

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:01.970
something—cities are natural.

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:07.140
Humans will, given time and technological
advancement, always form into cities.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:12.440
The existence of cities can really only happen
when the plusses outweighs the minuses.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:17.080
Back before the food surplus there were few
advantages to urbanized living and a huge

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:22.270
disadvantage—a commute to farming land during
a time when walking was for most the only

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:23.720
transportation method.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:28.740
Today, the plusses have increased and keep
increasing to the point where day by day more

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:30.650
and more people live in cities.

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:35.160
A major advantage for the existence of cities
is the ability for different businesses to

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.500
locate near each other.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:41.060
Part of the reason this is advantageous is
that people come to cities to find jobs because

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:45.750
all these businesses are there and so, if
businesses want to hire the best people to

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:48.630
be the best, they have to be in cities.

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:52.870
It’s a bit of a chicken and the egg problem,
but nowadays, different cities tend to play

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:55.910
host to different clusters of businesses.

00:06:55.910 --> 00:07:00.910
For example, LA is a hub for the entertainment
industry, Boston is a hub for medical research,

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:05.440
Dusseldorf is a hub for the telecommunications
industry, and Singapore is a hub for the finance

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:06.440
industry.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:10.730
In addition to the ability to tap into a common
labor pool, businesses cluster because they

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:14.460
want easy access to other businesses.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:19.070
Telecommunications companies like Vodafone,
Deutsche Telekom, and AT&amp;T all exist as direct

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:22.850
competitors in Dusseldorf, but the reason
they are there is because other companies

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:25.680
exist there are well that they can work with.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:30.170
Also in Dusseldorf are companies like Nokia,
Google, and LG which each make phones that

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:33.070
the mobile service providers might stock in
their stores.

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:36.870
By all being in the same place, they can more
easily collaborate and therefore they can

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:38.900
run their businesses more efficiently.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:43.120
There’s a sort of economies of scale effect
when all sorts of companies are all packed

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:44.990
together in one place.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:50.250
Now, not every business is in a city because
there are plenty of cases where the minuses

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:52.080
outweighs the plusses.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.860
You just won’t see a car manufacturing plant
in downtown Manhattan because it doesn’t

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:57.860
make sense.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:01.790
While there would be advantages such as access
to a higher skilled labor pool, proximity

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:06.260
to other businesses, and reduced transport
costs by being closer to the final market,

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:09.330
there are significant disadvantages.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:14.980
Land costs about $38 per square foot in New
York so if Tesla, for example, wanted to move

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:20.020
their factory to the city it would cost over
$200 million in land alone.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:24.860
The benefits would never outweigh the costs
and, in fact, it was this very problem that

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:26.600
led to the decline of Detroit.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:31.220
The city was a major center of automobile
manufacturing, but eventually manufacturers

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:35.969
figured out that they could greatly reduce
cost by moving the plants out of the city.

00:08:35.969 --> 00:08:40.719
Without a major industry to employ individuals,
many moved away and the population has steadily

00:08:40.719 --> 00:08:43.469
declined for the last few decades.

00:08:43.469 --> 00:08:45.839
Cities exist because they are efficient.

00:08:45.839 --> 00:08:50.189
Nobody’s forcing individuals to move to
cities, but billions have.

00:08:50.189 --> 00:08:54.050
Just imagine there were only ten people in
existence and one product that each of them

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:55.290
wanted to buy.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:59.920
If they were all equally spread out across
this plane, the logical location for a distribution

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:02.000
hub would be right in the center.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:06.269
Some individuals would happen to be close
to the hub by chance, but overall the product

00:09:06.269 --> 00:09:10.370
would have to be shipped a large distance
to get to every consumer.

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:15.060
Now lets say that eight of those ten individuals
lived together in a mini-city.

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:19.629
The logical location for the distribution
hub would then be right by the city.

00:09:19.629 --> 00:09:24.300
Shipping for the rural individuals would be
slow and expensive but overall the total shipping

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:27.279
distance and cost to individuals would be
lower.

00:09:27.279 --> 00:09:31.060
You can see this concept with where Amazon
puts their warehouses in the UK.

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:36.129
They could have one enormous warehouse at
the geographic center of the country and save

00:09:36.129 --> 00:09:39.069
some money through economies of scale, but
they don’t.

00:09:39.069 --> 00:09:43.810
They position each of their warehouses near
major population centers to reduce the total

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:45.209
shipping distance.

00:09:45.209 --> 00:09:49.329
In our modern context this concept is most
easily demonstrated through shipping, but

00:09:49.329 --> 00:09:54.730
historically individuals had to purchase nearly
all their goods at physical marketplaces.

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:58.589
Even just 200 years ago walking was the primary
means of transportation.

00:09:58.589 --> 00:10:04.330
In an hour nowadays we can drive 70 miles
while in an hour 200 years ago individuals

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:09.660
could walk only three miles so the push to
live closer to marketplaces was even higher.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:14.569
This is why suburbanization is a new concept,
but still more individuals than ever choose

00:10:14.569 --> 00:10:16.260
to live in cities.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:21.509
Division and specialization of labor was crucial
to the development of our world.

00:10:21.509 --> 00:10:23.509
Imagine you made everything you used.

00:10:23.509 --> 00:10:28.129
You grew your food, you gathered and filtered
your water, you built you own car.

00:10:28.129 --> 00:10:33.329
Just imagine how long it would take you to
build a car from scratch—at least a decade—and

00:10:33.329 --> 00:10:38.149
yet the average individual in the US can buy
a brand new car with the money they earn in

00:10:38.149 --> 00:10:39.420
about six months.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:43.209
That’s because there are people better at
making cars than you.

00:10:43.209 --> 00:10:47.160
There are some people who focus solely on
mining iron then some individuals who focus

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:51.740
solely on making glass and so there are thousands
of people producing all the different inputs

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:55.440
that go into making your car in the most efficient
way possible.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.170
Let’s say that you, for example, individually
can make a pencil in 60 minutes and paper

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:05.360
in 30 while another individual can make a
pencil in 30 minutes and paper in 60.

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:09.870
In a closed off world it would take each of
you 90 minutes to make a pencil and paper,

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:14.389
but let’s say you make two pieces of paper,
which would take you an hour, and the other

00:11:14.389 --> 00:11:18.209
person would make two pencils, which would
take them an hour, and then you each trade.

00:11:18.209 --> 00:11:22.069
Neither of you have given up anything, you
get exactly what you would have gotten by

00:11:22.069 --> 00:11:25.790
working independently, but you have each gained
time.

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:28.709
This is exactly why trade is beneficial.

00:11:28.709 --> 00:11:33.210
Certain people are better at making certain
things, so by everyone specializing in what

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:36.870
they are good at the entire world gets more
without giving more.

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:37.870
This is how efficiency happens.

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:41.850
Cities make this trade easier which leads
to more of it happening and therefore cities

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:43.329
are efficient.

00:11:43.329 --> 00:11:48.160
Humans naturally want to find the path of
least resistance and, with our spacial patterns,

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:51.700
the path of least resistance is to all live
together.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:55.600
Of course rural life will always exist and
needs to exist, but if you were to have a

00:11:55.600 --> 00:12:01.279
hand to pick up and organize every human into
the most efficient pattern possible, this

00:12:01.279 --> 00:12:02.730
is what it would look like.

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:08.139
Cities don’t create wealth and wealth doesn’t
create cities, but rather cities make wealth

00:12:08.139 --> 00:12:09.240
possible.

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:14.500
Cities are efficient and efficiency creates
wealth, and so people create cities.

